# pf-vpn-jail.conf -- loaded INSIDE the vpn jail (its /etc/pf.conf).
#
# The vpn jail is the router for qbittorrent/sonarr/radarr: their default
# route is this jail. Three jobs, all measured on a clean FreeBSD 15.1 install:
#
#  1. Translate their traffic onto the tunnel address. A WireGuard server only
#     accepts packets from the address it assigned to this peer; forwarded
#     as-is (source = the confined jail's own bridge address) every provider
#     drops them, so without this line the tunnel comes up and the download
#     apps still have no internet.
#  2. Never forward their traffic anywhere except into the tunnel. When wg0 is
#     down its routes vanish and this jail would send their packets out its
#     ordinary interface instead -- with the real address. Dropped here, and
#     again on the host (pf-killswitch.conf.tmpl) in case this ruleset is
#     ever not loaded.
#
#  3. Clamp the TCP segment size to what fits in the tunnel. wg0's MTU is
#     1420 (both writers of wg0.conf set it) while the confined jails
#     advertise segments sized for 1500; path-MTU discovery through a VPN
#     provider rarely works, so without this small requests succeed and
#     anything large stalls forever (measured: a 9 MB download hung, and the
#     nightly package upgrade of a confined jail with it). 1380 = 1420 - 40.
#
# pf only loads in a jail whose securelevel is below 2; the installer lowers
# this one jail to 1. "(wg0)" is resolved at packet time, so the rules load
# before the tunnel exists.

scrub on wg0 all max-mss 1380
nat on wg0 inet from { {{VPN_CONFINED_IPS}} } to any -> (wg0)
block drop out quick on vnet0 from { {{VPN_CONFINED_IPS}} } to any
